#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493003} :activity/id #uuid "3866c2aa-e5f1-566e-84b6-7cace07a50a6" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048350 :topic/id #uuid "97af7835-46ed-5be6-8581-1612329913e7" :topic/math-academy-id 1132} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492971 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492928 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6f31a7-12c7-533a-a616-32df997a304f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492237}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492237} :answer-field/id #uuid "6377b71b-3301-54ad-85e4-81211618a817" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492747} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d14f365-3431-51a2-85f1-fb6aa4f3083e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492273}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492273} :answer-field/id #uuid "d11afbdb-9351-5d55-af3d-0ebc6b7799e3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492790}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdc8ed74-44a5-549d-abf7-39e6c995f550" :question/math-academy-id "q-251773" :question/problem "![](images/75/7515041ccdceb8d13e4e718bda12af7535cfbb4a37b0180b04f88a08db4947b2.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(3,-1)$ and passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(3,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,\\frac{5}{4}),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=\\frac{5}{4}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-3)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{4}+1) \\\\ 9 & =4p(\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\ p & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(y+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4(0+1) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-3 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $1$  and $5.$"} :step/id #uuid "96d654a5-c93d-56e0-932c-bfd42b4b3f25" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492993}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492979 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441439 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3326bfbc-602b-5b97-a232-910eb4540d34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441399} :answer-field/id #uuid "78edd9c5-7539-5d54-8eb1-437fb325e3d3" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441425} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c1fd71a3-f959-5632-b94a-c770e9c6d2a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186441414} :answer-field/id #uuid "91e3b777-9db3-51a5-ab92-8bddd54cd065" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186441428}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a25c3b40-7f3b-57d8-a341-b7f66aac34c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-251826" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e95f9087b5b607f2d33d49c0f5db123eede589e53adc94747f3a33c1fe68f90.png)\n\n**From top to bottom**, the **exact** values of the $y$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-8,-1)$ and passes through the point $(-6,1).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(-8,-1)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(y+1)^{2}=4p(x+8).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-6,1),$ we substitute $x=-6$ and $y=1$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1+1)^{2} & =4p(-6+8) \\\\ 4 & =4p(2) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(x+8).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y+1)^{2} & =2(0+8) \\\\ (y+1)^{2} & =16 \\\\ y+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{16} \\\\ y & =\\pm 4-1 \\\\ y & =-5,3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from top to bottom, the $y$-intercepts are $3$ and  $-5.$"} :step/id #uuid "a55bc8a3-ad49-5dfc-b4d0-974e21a65508"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492993 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492872 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "608d4538-0b4d-5182-94b8-be0b177bf697" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492412}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492412} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b765fc4-4996-5266-90d9-3a4e2f7f9f25" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492734} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c48e924c-701d-5314-92e8-98bc2d3981c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492609}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492609} :answer-field/id #uuid "933aac48-b970-5148-a237-e6565ad9de8d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "735e4f99-1ffc-57e4-b137-966d99c9aba8" :question/math-academy-id "q-251783" :question/problem "![](images/7a/7ac752bbc51ea08ba8723fa0aba111d3f302cc149db8762208ae18ed31be02d1.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the values of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}\nand \n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(1,-4)$ and passes through the point $(0,-3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(1,-4)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(0,-3),$ we substitute $x=0$ and $y=-3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-1)^{2} & =4p(-3+4) \\\\ 1 & =4p(1) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =0+4 \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4 \\\\ x-1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{4} \\\\ x & =\\pm 2+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1$  and $3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ca9516a2-5640-5521-9384-7fc046858e73" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492979}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493003 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492924 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9f61033c-bec0-5a9b-aaf9-ae37b563257c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492532} {:answer/id #uuid "a4366df5-83ab-5bf1-a218-b427953b47ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492547} {:answer/id #uuid "b078e7a8-a8b1-5f0a-8d3b-7817778a5e09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492570} {:answer/id #uuid "c192c3f6-fee4-5de2-baf8-b83ae6c0b752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492603} {:answer/id #uuid "e6374016-52a5-5c7e-89fb-b178fcb7aab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492663}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492532} :answer-field/id #uuid "b313a6a7-5aef-5eef-9fdc-9a3f6384635e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492777}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f789e17e-9222-50f7-90e6-3a489f3b51f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-79303" :question/problem "![](images/84/84fa3bc1d70144b0fd2d1865e784ed1cee9d8b83633547f2e62be1a30e4242aa.png)\n\nWhat is the $y$-intercept of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(2,-5)$ and passes through the point $(-2,3).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex at $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k),\n$$\n\nand substituting $(h,k)=(2,-5)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}=4p(y+5).\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,3),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=3$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2-2)^{2} & =4p(3+5) \\\\ 16 & =4p(8) \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-2)^{2} & =2(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $y$-axis, we must have $x=0.$ So, we substitute $x=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(0-2)^{2} & =2(y+5) \\\\ 4 & =2(y+5) \\\\ y & =2-5 \\\\ y & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $y$-intercept is $-3.$"} :step/id #uuid "ec4c1f2e-ee02-534f-8b4d-4d02bf4356e6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493005}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186493005 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492926 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "168bd404-2959-5371-bc5c-7fbba3473aa1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1+\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492259}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492259} :answer-field/id #uuid "34e53936-bbb9-5fb0-9b7e-e35958cdf4b9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492726} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46bda047-ac5b-540d-b339-64ed2abef508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1-\\sqrt{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492357}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492357} :answer-field/id #uuid "b06a318c-820e-5ff0-97ca-8c71655f9348" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492775}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f822983e-1d14-50b6-989d-68e7f1c76860" :question/math-academy-id "q-240667" :question/problem "![](images/ac/ac9ee9825678ad3ba815204c39ac2fe4ea85972cc60458251fcc9e9fdb4ba0f7.png)\n\n**From left to right**, the **exact values** of the $x$-intercepts of the parabola shown above are{{field-1}}and{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the equation of the parabola. From the diagram, we see that the parabola has a vertex at $(-1,3)$ and passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}).$\n\nThe general standard equation of an up- or down-opening parabola with vertex $(h,k)$ is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $(h,k)=(-1,3)$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find $p.$ Since the parabola passes through the point $(-2,\\frac{5}{2}),$ we substitute $x=-2$ and $y=\\frac{5}{2}$ into the equation and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-2+1)^{2} & =4p(\\frac{5}{2}-3) \\\\ (-1)^{2} & =4p(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 1 & =-2p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(y-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen the parabola intersects the $x$-axis, we must have $y=0.$ So, we substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the parabola and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =-2(0-3) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =6 \\\\ x+1 & =\\pm \\sqrt{6} \\\\ x & =-1\\pm \\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, from left to right, the $x$-intercepts are $-1-\\sqrt{6}$ and $-1+\\sqrt{6}.$"} :step/id #uuid "f42eea9a-325b-5c56-95b4-dc3b76529b04" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186492971}}] :activity/title "Calculating Intercepts of Parabolas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186492207}]}
